my name is Jennifer Doudna I'm a  professor at the University of  California Berkeley and a Howard Hughes  Medical Institute investigator  my research involves studying how  bacteria fight viral infection and in  the course of that work which was a  project to discover fundamentally an  aspect of science that nobody had  investigated previously we found out  that a protein called cast 9 which is  part of the CRISPR bacterial immune  system is an enzyme that can find and  cut DNA sequences in cells it's a  molecule shown in this model in the  white part of the model that holds on to  double-stranded DNA and makes a cut just  like a pair of scissors and it can be  programmed by bacteria or by scientists  to find a particular DNA sequence and  make a break and that's important  because when that occurs in an animal or  plant cell the cell will repair the DNA  and in the process make a change to the  DNA sequence and this has been the  advent of a very exciting technology for  genome editing a way that we can alter  DNA in cells precisely and simply using  this tool in ways that in the future are  likely to lead to cures for genetic  diseases as well as lots of new insights  into the fundamental aspects of genetics  in biology  crisper bass gene-editing gives  scientists a tool for manipulating the  DNA in cells that's unprecedented we can  now understand the fundamental genetics  of any organism at a level that  previously was impossible really even to  imagine lots of people think about the  use of CRISPR and similar technologies  to cure genetic disease in humans I  think one of the exciting profound  advances that will happen in the future  actually has to do with understanding  plants and figuring out how how plants  live in their environment why plants  have the traits that they do plant  genomes are typically much much larger  than the human genome and with the  CRISPR tools we now can manipulate plant  genomes to understand the content of  that DNA and how the traits in plants  are encoded in DNA and can be  manipulated now in the laboratory using  this kind of technology so I think we're  going to see a revolution in  understanding our natural world by the  use of CRISPR based genome editing
 

